WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:08.123
If three kinds of taste cell send the same chemical signal, how does the brain tell

2
00:00:08.123 --> 00:00:09.123
them apart?

3
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:13.547
In the taste bud, type II cells detect sweet, umami and bitter.

4
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.780
Sweet and umami cells carry T1R receptors.

5
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.840
Bitter cells carry T2R receptors.

6
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.740
After the receptor, all three use the same steps.

7
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.500
An enzyme, PLCβ2, leads to a calcium rise.

8
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.220
Calcium opens a channel, and the cell depolarizes.

9
00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:37.790
Then ATP flows out through a channel onto the nerve fibre.

10
00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.330
So the signal is the same.

11
00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:44.590
The rule's answer: what differs is which cell the nerve listens to.

12
00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:49.390
Zhang and colleagues deleted PLCβ2 from the whole mouse.

13
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.280
Nerve responses to the tested sweet, amino-acid and bitter stimuli were lost.

14
00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:00.660
In lick tests, sweet preference was strongly reduced, and bitter avoidance was lost.

15
00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:07.063
Sour and salty responses remained, because those cells use other routes.

16
00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:10.927
Then they put it back only in cells with bitter receptors.

17
00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:13.610
Which responses come back?

18
00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:15.890
Bitter only, all three, or none?

19
00:01:21.933 --> 00:01:24.453
Bitter only, in the nerve and in behaviour.

20
00:01:25.293 --> 00:01:28.133
Sweet and amino-acid responses did not return.

21
00:01:29.117 --> 00:01:32.097
The shared enzyme works only for its own cell's quality.

22
00:01:32.957 --> 00:01:33.957
The lines are separate.

23
00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.490
Does the cell also decide what a line means?

24
00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:39.730
Put a receptor in the wrong cell.

25
00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:45.030
Mueller and colleagues put a human bitter receptor into the sweet cells of mice.

26
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.600
Its ligand is a bitter compound that normal mice ignore.

27
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.260
Will these mice avoid it, or drink it?

28
00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:00.730
They drink it, while normal mice stay indifferent.

29
00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:05.730
The receptor detects the molecule, but the sweet cell decides what that means.

30
00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:10.030
In mouse taste, each kind of cell acts like a labeled line.

31
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.100
One consequence: a bitter cell pools many receptors.

32
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.660
By its rate alone, that cell cannot say which bitter compound it met.

33
00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:24.273
Not every bitter cell carries the same receptors, so the pathway may keep some

34
00:02:24.273 --> 00:02:29.733
distinctions. Still, pooling fits a sense that mostly answers: eat it, or reject it?

35
00:02:30.617 --> 00:02:34.477
Keep the scope. These are mouse experiments on the periphery.

36
00:02:35.477 --> 00:02:38.957
Some taste cells and nerve cells respond to more than one quality.

37
00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:42.407
Smell reads a pattern across many lines.

38
00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:45.527
Taste reads mainly which kind of cell is active.
